<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="Syntax">
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`refuse.named(name)
refuse.named(name, status, message)
refuse.named(name, status, message, { schema })`} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Members">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>refuse.named</code></td><td><code>{'<Name extends string>(name: Name, status?: number, message?: string) => ((data?: undefined) => Failed<Name, undefined>) & { is: (error: unknown) => error is Failed<Name, undefined> }'}</code></td><td>Declares a named, narrowable failure. The status defaults to 400 and the message to that status's phrase. The factory carries <code>is</code>, which is the only narrowing available where there is no <code>Reactive</code> in scope.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>refuse.named</code></td><td><code>{'<Name extends string, Data extends JsonValue>(name: Name, status: number | undefined, message: string | ((data: Data) => string) | undefined, options: { schema: Schema<Data> }) => ((data?: Data) => Failed<Name, Data>) & { is: (error: unknown) => error is Failed<Name, Data> }'}</code></td><td>The same, carrying data. The schema is the only way to declare it, so <code>Data</code> and the name are both inferred and the name is written once. A <code>message</code> given as a function runs at construction on the data, a <code>Failed</code> being a value rather than a thing with getters.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Failed&lt;Name, Data&gt;</code></td><td><code>{'Error & { name: Name; status: number; message: string; data: Data }'}</code></td><td>The one refusal type, in process and over a wire alike. Structural, because a caller catches a shape rather than a class it imported.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>return myError(data)</code></td><td><code>Failed&lt;Name, Data&gt;</code></td><td>One spelling for every refusal: a handler returns them. Returning refuses.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>notFound</code></td><td><code>{'(data?: { path: string; method: string }) => Failed<\'NotFound\', { path: string; method: string }>'}</code></td><td>abide's own, returned where no route matched and where no handler is mounted at that address and method.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>validationError</code></td><td><code>&lt;T&gt;(data?: Issues&lt;T&gt;) =&gt; Failed&lt;'ValidationError', Issues&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code></td><td>At 422, returned where a schema refuses.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>aborted</code></td><td><code>(data?: undefined) =&gt; Failed&lt;'Aborted', undefined&gt;</code></td><td>At 499, where every call waiting on a load handed a <code>signal</code> and all of them aborted. The value keeps what it held.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>myError(data)</code> discarded</td><td>compile error</td><td>A <code>Failed</code> built and thrown away is a refusal that did not happen, so an expression statement whose type is <code>Failed</code> is refused, naming both repairs.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Description">
        <p><strong>Construction is inert.</strong> Building a <code>Failed</code> does not throw,
        which lets a gate return one and puts the failure in a handler's return type honestly. The
        cost is that a built-and-dropped refusal would fall through silently, which is why
        discarding one is a compile error rather than a lint.</p>

        <p><strong>Three named refusals and no more.</strong> A name is public surface forever. The
        rest of what abide raises — 403 on an origin mismatch, 413 over-size, 504 on a timeout — has
        no data to narrow to, so it stays the undeclared <code>HttpError</code> at its status that
        <code>refuse(status)</code> means.</p>

        <p><strong>On a wire it is Problem Details (RFC 9457).</strong> An answer refusing is
        <code>application/problem+json</code> at the refusal's own status: its name is the
        <code>type</code>, its message the <code>detail</code>, and its data a <code>data</code>
        member. An undeclared <code>HttpError</code> is <code>type: "about:blank"</code> titled by
        its reason phrase, as the RFC has it (15.21). A browser's <code>Rpc</code> rebuilds the same
        <code>Failed</code> from it, so <code>isError</code> narrows by name there exactly as in
        process; any other client reads a format it already knows.</p>

        <p>A declared refusal defaults to 400 because it is by construction an expected answer, and
        500 is the one status certainly wrong for it: a generated client reads it as a server fault,
        and a model retries instead of re-planning.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Examples">
        <Section title="Declaring a refusal" level={3}>
            <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`export const notMember = refuse.named('NotMember', 403, undefined, {
    schema: z.object({ group: z.string() }),
})`} />
        </Section>

        <Section title="Returning a refusal" level={3}>
            <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`export const invite = POST(async ({ group }: { group: string }) => {
    if (!(await isMember(group))) return notMember({ group })
    return send(group)
})`} />
        </Section>

        <Section title="Narrowing a refusal" level={3}>
            <Code language="ts" caption="browser" code={`if (invite.isError(error, 'NotMember')) show(error.data.group)`} />
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="See also">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a></li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a></li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/reactive')}><code>Reactive</code></a></li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
